# Why this patch exists:
# Upstream bug: `toMajorUnitNumber()` used to coerce Medusa's BigNumber-
# shaped amount to a plain JS number and return it unchanged, regardless of
# shape.
#
# This has gone through two rounds of investigation (see project memory /
# session notes for the full traces):
#
# Round 1: confirmed against SumUp's API that the unpatched code sent an
# intended £11.00 charge as `amount: 1100` (i.e. £1,100.00 by SumUp's own
# accounting) — a 100x overcharge. That fix divided by the currency's
# decimal-digit factor (via @medusajs/framework/utils's `defaultCurrencies`)
# at all three call sites that funnel through this helper: checkout creation
# (createCheckoutPayload), refunds (refundPayment), and the cart-total-
# changed reconciliation path (updatePayment).
#
# Round 2: that division introduced the *opposite* bug — investigated
# directly against a real Medusa v2.21 cart -> checkout -> SumUp flow (not a
# synthetic test): a real £645.00 cart was submitted to SumUp as £6.45, and
# a live refund attempt confirmed the same problem on that path too. Traced
# to @medusajs/payment's PaymentModuleService: `createPaymentSession` /
# `updatePaymentSession` pass a plain JS `number` already in *major* units
# (e.g. `645`, not `64500`), and `refundPaymentFromProvider_` passes
# `refund.raw_amount`, a BigNumber-raw object (`{ value: "645", precision:
# 20 }`). Per @medusajs/utils's own `BigNumber` class, `.raw.value`,
# `.numeric`, `.bigNumber.toNumber()`, and a plain number all represent the
# *same* major-unit decimal amount in this Medusa version — `precision` is
# significant-digit precision for arbitrary-precision math, not a scale
# factor. No shape Medusa v2.21 hands this provider, on any of the three
# call sites, ever represents minor units (pence/cents), so the Round 1 fix
# was wrong for everything we could reach — hence the 100x UNDERcharge.
#
# Fix: extract the numeric value from whatever shape arrives and stop
# scaling it — only round to the currency's decimal digits for display/API
# formatting. As a backstop against a *future* regression (e.g. some other
# call path, or a future Medusa version, handing this minor units again —
# which is exactly how the Round 1 bug manifested), throw if the resulting
# amount is implausibly large for a retail transaction (over 50,000 in the
# currency's major units) rather than silently submitting it — that
# magnitude is the signature a minor-unit leak leaves once mistakenly
# treated as major units.
#
# Not yet reported upstream. Safe to delete this patch (and its entry in
# pnpm-workspace.yaml / pnpm-lock.yaml) once upstream ships a fix and the
# dependency is upgraded past it — but re-verify against a real cart/refund
# flow first, the same way this fix was, rather than trusting either
# direction on inspection alone.
